1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,452
I wrote twelve prompt injection attacks and a keyword filter to stop

2
00:00:04,452 --> 00:00:06,374
them. The filter stopped three.

3
00:00:06,374 --> 00:00:07,801
With nothing in place,

4
00:00:07,801 --> 00:00:09,997
all twelve stole the private record.

5
00:00:09,997 --> 00:00:11,974
And with a simple design change,

6
00:00:11,974 --> 00:00:14,280
the model was fooled all twelve times,

7
00:00:14,280 --> 00:00:16,146
but nothing left the building.

8
00:00:16,496 --> 00:00:19,282
That is the lesson of this security chapter.

9
00:00:19,282 --> 00:00:20,849
Filters are bypassable.

10
00:00:20,849 --> 00:00:22,184
Architecture holds.

11
00:00:22,184 --> 00:00:24,157
We will also test permissions,

12
00:00:24,157 --> 00:00:27,117
personal data and data leaks between customers.

13
00:00:27,117 --> 00:00:30,309
Every result comes from a toy you can run yourself.

14
00:00:31,559 --> 00:00:34,997
Welcome to lesson ten of the cloud for A I engineers course,

15
00:00:34,997 --> 00:00:36,340
on The Code Notebook.

16
00:00:36,690 --> 00:00:39,825
The handwritten notes and the code for this lesson are linked in

17
00:00:39,825 --> 00:00:40,747
the description.

18
00:00:41,997 --> 00:00:43,034
We have built,

19
00:00:43,034 --> 00:00:45,684
trained, served and shipped an A I system.

20
00:00:45,684 --> 00:00:47,816
Now someone will try to break it,

21
00:00:47,816 --> 00:00:50,755
or just make an honest mistake that leaks data.

22
00:00:50,755 --> 00:00:52,945
Security for A I has one surprise.

23
00:00:52,945 --> 00:00:56,863
Most of the damage in real incidents came from the system around

24
00:00:56,863 --> 00:00:57,669
the model,

25
00:00:57,669 --> 00:00:59,110
not the model itself.

26
00:00:59,460 --> 00:01:00,594
Honest labels.

27
00:01:00,594 --> 00:01:05,006
The injection test uses a deliberately gullible scripted stand in,

28
00:01:05,006 --> 00:01:06,141
and fake data.

29
00:01:06,141 --> 00:01:10,805
The permission checker is my simplified version of cloud policy rules.

30
00:01:10,805 --> 00:01:14,271
The personal data test uses thirty made up strings.

31
00:01:14,271 --> 00:01:17,297
None of it is a benchmark of a real product.

32
00:01:18,547 --> 00:01:20,452
Start with who is responsible.

33
00:01:20,452 --> 00:01:22,974
The cloud provider secures the buildings,

34
00:01:22,974 --> 00:01:23,927
the machines,

35
00:01:23,927 --> 00:01:25,160
the model hosting,

36
00:01:25,160 --> 00:01:27,907
and promises not to train on your A P I data.

37
00:01:27,907 --> 00:01:30,541
You secure everything about how you use it.

38
00:01:30,541 --> 00:01:32,054
Who may call the model,

39
00:01:32,054 --> 00:01:33,287
what data goes in,

40
00:01:33,287 --> 00:01:35,193
what the answers are used for.

41
00:01:35,543 --> 00:01:36,960
There are five locks.

42
00:01:36,960 --> 00:01:38,207
Identity, network,

43
00:01:38,207 --> 00:01:39,567
encryption, logging,

44
00:01:39,567 --> 00:01:41,494
and application authorisation.

45
00:01:41,494 --> 00:01:42,515
A mnemonic is,

46
00:01:42,515 --> 00:01:43,762
I need every lock,

47
00:01:43,762 --> 00:01:46,596
always. The provider hands you the first four.

48
00:01:46,596 --> 00:01:47,389
The fifth,

49
00:01:47,389 --> 00:01:48,693
you build yourself.

50
00:01:49,943 --> 00:01:52,440
Here is the gap that catches teams out.

51
00:01:52,440 --> 00:01:56,272
Identity and access management decides who may call the model.

52
00:01:56,272 --> 00:01:59,640
A private network decides which pipe the call travels.

53
00:01:59,640 --> 00:02:02,311
A guardrail looks for attacks in the text.

54
00:02:02,661 --> 00:02:04,783
But nothing asks the real question.

55
00:02:04,783 --> 00:02:06,688
May this user send this prompt,

56
00:02:06,688 --> 00:02:07,885
see this document,

57
00:02:07,885 --> 00:02:09,083
or fire this tool?

58
00:02:09,083 --> 00:02:11,477
That check is application authorisation,

59
00:02:11,477 --> 00:02:12,838
it sits in your code,

60
00:02:12,838 --> 00:02:16,430
and most of the incidents in this lesson come from missing it.

61
00:02:17,680 --> 00:02:18,810
Identity first.

62
00:02:18,810 --> 00:02:22,083
Your service needs to prove who it is to the cloud.

63
00:02:22,083 --> 00:02:23,749
The best way is keyless.

64
00:02:23,749 --> 00:02:24,820
On Kubernetes,

65
00:02:24,820 --> 00:02:27,855
the cluster hands each pod a short lived token,

66
00:02:27,855 --> 00:02:31,663
which the cloud exchanges for short lived cloud credentials.

67
00:02:31,663 --> 00:02:33,924
Nothing long lived exists to leak.

68
00:02:34,274 --> 00:02:36,652
All three big clouds work this way,

69
00:02:36,652 --> 00:02:38,176
with different names.

70
00:02:38,176 --> 00:02:39,761
Pod Identity on A W S,

71
00:02:39,761 --> 00:02:42,322
Workload Identity on Google and Azure.

72
00:02:42,322 --> 00:02:44,334
Prefer it over keys in files,

73
00:02:44,334 --> 00:02:45,249
every time.

74
00:02:46,499 --> 00:02:50,730
Least privilege means giving a role only the permissions it needs.

75
00:02:50,730 --> 00:02:54,176
I wrote a small policy checker with simplified rules.

76
00:02:54,176 --> 00:02:56,474
Anything is denied unless allowed,

77
00:02:56,474 --> 00:02:58,469
an explicit deny always wins,

78
00:02:58,469 --> 00:03:00,222
and stars match anything.

79
00:03:00,222 --> 00:03:02,157
This is my teaching version,

80
00:03:02,157 --> 00:03:03,970
not the real cloud engine.

81
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:07,006
I tested two roles with twelve requests.

82
00:03:07,006 --> 00:03:09,814
The narrow role is the notebook's example,

83
00:03:09,814 --> 00:03:13,293
allowed to invoke one family of models in one region.

84
00:03:13,293 --> 00:03:17,016
The broad role allows everything under the model service,

85
00:03:17,016 --> 00:03:18,359
on every resource.

86
00:03:18,359 --> 00:03:23,303
An organisation rule also blocks calls outside two approved European regions.

87
00:03:24,553 --> 00:03:27,835
The narrow role was allowed two of the twelve requests.

88
00:03:27,835 --> 00:03:29,782
Both were ordinary model calls.

89
00:03:29,782 --> 00:03:31,785
The broad role was allowed nine.

90
00:03:32,135 --> 00:03:35,187
Those extra seven included creating a guardrail,

91
00:03:35,187 --> 00:03:37,007
starting a fine tuning job,

92
00:03:37,007 --> 00:03:40,940
deleting a custom model and changing the logging configuration.

93
00:03:40,940 --> 00:03:44,462
A role that only needed to call a model could switch off

94
00:03:44,462 --> 00:03:45,870
its own audit trail.

95
00:03:45,870 --> 00:03:49,803
The organisation rule still blocked the other regions for both.

96
00:03:51,053 --> 00:03:52,017
Network next.

97
00:03:52,017 --> 00:03:56,041
Use private endpoints so calls to the model service never cross the

98
00:03:56,041 --> 00:03:57,174
public internet.

99
00:03:57,174 --> 00:03:58,931
And turn public access off.

100
00:03:58,931 --> 00:04:02,218
If you add a private path but leave the public one on,

101
00:04:02,218 --> 00:04:04,372
the private path is only optional.

102
00:04:04,722 --> 00:04:07,047
A stronger control is a perimeter.

103
00:04:07,047 --> 00:04:11,268
Google's service controls draw a boundary around the A I service,

104
00:04:11,268 --> 00:04:15,367
so even valid credentials cannot copy data or models out of it.

105
00:04:15,367 --> 00:04:17,753
It is an anti exfiltration control.

106
00:04:19,003 --> 00:04:23,789
Secrets. Keys for outside model providers and database passwords belong in a

107
00:04:23,789 --> 00:04:24,985
secrets manager.

108
00:04:24,985 --> 00:04:26,959
Never in an environment file,

109
00:04:26,959 --> 00:04:28,275
never in an image,

110
00:04:28,275 --> 00:04:29,831
and never in a prompt,

111
00:04:29,831 --> 00:04:33,061
because a model can repeat what is in its context.

112
00:04:33,411 --> 00:04:36,746
Use customer managed encryption keys for training data,

113
00:04:36,746 --> 00:04:37,763
custom models,

114
00:04:37,763 --> 00:04:39,289
vector stores and logs.

115
00:04:39,289 --> 00:04:41,550
They let you revoke access yourself,

116
00:04:41,550 --> 00:04:43,472
and they leave an audit trail.

117
00:04:44,722 --> 00:04:46,273
Logs come in two kinds,

118
00:04:46,273 --> 00:04:47,823
and people mix them up.

119
00:04:47,823 --> 00:04:51,154
The audit log records who called which A P I and when.

120
00:04:51,154 --> 00:04:53,222
It does not contain the prompts.

121
00:04:53,222 --> 00:04:56,553
A separate invocation log records prompts and answers,

122
00:04:56,553 --> 00:04:58,218
and it is off by default.

123
00:04:58,569 --> 00:04:59,986
Both edges are sharp.

124
00:04:59,986 --> 00:05:00,950
With no logs,

125
00:05:00,950 --> 00:05:02,537
you have no audit trail,

126
00:05:02,537 --> 00:05:04,635
and some regulations require one.

127
00:05:04,635 --> 00:05:05,598
With logs on,

128
00:05:05,598 --> 00:05:09,397
you have created a new store full of personal data and secrets.

129
00:05:09,397 --> 00:05:10,247
Encrypt it,

130
00:05:10,247 --> 00:05:11,381
restrict access,

131
00:05:11,381 --> 00:05:13,139
and set a retention period.

132
00:05:14,389 --> 00:05:16,117
What happens to your data?

133
00:05:16,117 --> 00:05:17,960
All three big clouds commit,

134
00:05:17,960 --> 00:05:18,882
by contract,

135
00:05:18,882 --> 00:05:22,339
not to train their foundation models on your A P I data.

136
00:05:22,339 --> 00:05:23,779
That part is settled.

137
00:05:23,779 --> 00:05:26,717
The open questions are retention and residency,

138
00:05:26,717 --> 00:05:28,964
and they differ feature by feature.

139
00:05:29,314 --> 00:05:32,882
Some features keep prompts for a while to detect abuse.

140
00:05:32,882 --> 00:05:34,757
Zero data retention exists,

141
00:05:34,757 --> 00:05:37,660
but you usually must opt in or get approval,

142
00:05:37,660 --> 00:05:39,717
and some features rule it out.

143
00:05:39,717 --> 00:05:41,410
Residency has traps too,

144
00:05:41,410 --> 00:05:44,857
such as endpoints that route requests across regions.

145
00:05:44,857 --> 00:05:48,910
Read the provider's page before you promise a customer EU only.

146
00:05:50,160 --> 00:05:51,231
Personal data,

147
00:05:51,231 --> 00:05:52,243
called P I I.

148
00:05:52,243 --> 00:05:53,969
Redact it at four places.

149
00:05:53,969 --> 00:05:55,814
Before embedding documents,

150
00:05:55,814 --> 00:05:59,207
because it is hard to find and delete inside vectors.

151
00:05:59,207 --> 00:06:00,279
On the prompt.

152
00:06:00,279 --> 00:06:01,350
On the output.

153
00:06:01,350 --> 00:06:02,719
And before logging.

154
00:06:03,069 --> 00:06:05,895
How good is a simple pattern based redactor?

155
00:06:05,895 --> 00:06:07,779
I wrote thirty test strings,

156
00:06:07,779 --> 00:06:09,192
with made up people.

157
00:06:09,192 --> 00:06:11,841
Emails, phone numbers in several formats,

158
00:06:11,841 --> 00:06:14,078
card numbers with a checksum test,

159
00:06:14,078 --> 00:06:16,198
names, addresses and ID numbers.

160
00:06:16,198 --> 00:06:19,318
Then I measured how many personal items it found.

161
00:06:20,568 --> 00:06:23,924
Patterns did fairly well on things with a fixed shape.

162
00:06:23,924 --> 00:06:25,834
Emails, eighty-three percent.

163
00:06:25,834 --> 00:06:26,875
Phone numbers,

164
00:06:26,875 --> 00:06:28,553
eighty-six. Card numbers,

165
00:06:28,553 --> 00:06:31,793
eighty. They missed formats like at example dot com,

166
00:06:31,793 --> 00:06:33,124
and spoken numbers.

167
00:06:33,474 --> 00:06:34,274
On names,

168
00:06:34,274 --> 00:06:37,226
addresses and ID numbers they found nothing,

169
00:06:37,226 --> 00:06:38,333
zero. Overall,

170
00:06:38,333 --> 00:06:39,809
fifty-eight percent.

171
00:06:39,809 --> 00:06:43,746
It also raised false alarms on two of four harmless strings.

172
00:06:43,746 --> 00:06:46,268
Real systems add trained recognisers,

173
00:06:46,268 --> 00:06:47,559
such as Presidio,

174
00:06:47,559 --> 00:06:50,327
Comprehend and Sensitive Data Protection,

175
00:06:50,327 --> 00:06:51,865
which I did not test.

176
00:06:53,115 --> 00:06:55,342
A word on confidential computing.

177
00:06:55,342 --> 00:06:58,293
It protects data while it is being processed,

178
00:06:58,293 --> 00:07:03,230
by running inside encrypted hardware that even the cloud operator cannot read.

179
00:07:03,230 --> 00:07:06,722
Azure and Google offer H one hundred machines with it.

180
00:07:07,072 --> 00:07:08,080
It has limits.

181
00:07:08,080 --> 00:07:10,320
It is weak against physical attacks,

182
00:07:10,320 --> 00:07:14,128
and multi GPU support was limited when the notebook was written.

183
00:07:14,128 --> 00:07:17,937
And it does nothing about prompt injection or application leaks.

184
00:07:17,937 --> 00:07:20,289
It is a complement to the other locks,

185
00:07:20,289 --> 00:07:21,521
not a replacement.

186
00:07:22,771 --> 00:07:24,787
There is a standard checklist,

187
00:07:24,787 --> 00:07:28,226
the O W A S P top ten for language model applications.

188
00:07:28,226 --> 00:07:30,302
Prompt injection is number one.

189
00:07:30,302 --> 00:07:32,792
Then sensitive information disclosure,

190
00:07:32,792 --> 00:07:33,800
supply chain,

191
00:07:33,800 --> 00:07:35,520
data and model poisoning,

192
00:07:35,520 --> 00:07:37,239
improper output handling,

193
00:07:37,239 --> 00:07:38,485
excessive agency,

194
00:07:38,485 --> 00:07:40,026
system prompt leakage,

195
00:07:40,026 --> 00:07:42,161
vector and embedding weaknesses,

196
00:07:42,161 --> 00:07:44,889
misinformation, and unbounded consumption.

197
00:07:45,239 --> 00:07:48,205
Notice how many of these are about the system,

198
00:07:48,205 --> 00:07:49,272
not the model.

199
00:07:49,272 --> 00:07:50,459
Output handling,

200
00:07:50,459 --> 00:07:51,645
agency, vectors,

201
00:07:51,645 --> 00:07:56,034
consumption. That matches what the notebook says about real incidents.

202
00:07:56,034 --> 00:07:58,170
Architecture beats model tuning.

203
00:07:59,420 --> 00:08:01,744
Prompt injection comes in two kinds.

204
00:08:01,744 --> 00:08:04,359
Direct is the user typing something like,

205
00:08:04,359 --> 00:08:06,044
ignore your instructions.

206
00:08:06,044 --> 00:08:07,729
The attacker is the user,

207
00:08:07,729 --> 00:08:11,157
and the harm is a bypassed policy in their own session.

208
00:08:11,507 --> 00:08:13,546
Indirect is far more dangerous.

209
00:08:13,546 --> 00:08:16,982
The instruction is hidden in something the model reads.

210
00:08:16,982 --> 00:08:17,855
A web page,

211
00:08:17,855 --> 00:08:18,612
an email,

212
00:08:18,612 --> 00:08:19,311
a P D F,

213
00:08:19,311 --> 00:08:20,476
a search result,

214
00:08:20,476 --> 00:08:21,757
a calendar invite,

215
00:08:21,757 --> 00:08:22,922
a tool's output.

216
00:08:22,922 --> 00:08:24,844
The attacker is not the user.

217
00:08:24,844 --> 00:08:26,417
The user is the victim.

218
00:08:27,667 --> 00:08:29,995
How does stolen data actually leave?

219
00:08:29,995 --> 00:08:32,556
One well known path is a markdown image.

220
00:08:32,556 --> 00:08:36,107
The injected text tells the model to write an image link,

221
00:08:36,107 --> 00:08:38,610
with the secret inside the web address.

222
00:08:38,610 --> 00:08:41,520
The chat window loads the image automatically,

223
00:08:41,520 --> 00:08:43,848
and the secret goes to the attacker.

224
00:08:43,848 --> 00:08:45,012
No click needed.

225
00:08:45,362 --> 00:08:46,264
Other paths.

226
00:08:46,264 --> 00:08:48,463
A link the user is nudged to click.

227
00:08:48,463 --> 00:08:50,492
And any tool that talks outward,

228
00:08:50,492 --> 00:08:51,507
such as email,

229
00:08:51,507 --> 00:08:52,409
a web fetch,

230
00:08:52,409 --> 00:08:53,931
or posting to a ticket.

231
00:08:53,931 --> 00:08:56,073
A real case in twenty twenty-five,

232
00:08:56,073 --> 00:08:57,200
called EchoLeak,

233
00:08:57,200 --> 00:09:00,977
used a hidden instruction in an email to make an assistant leak

234
00:09:00,977 --> 00:09:02,330
data with no clicks.

235
00:09:03,580 --> 00:09:05,210
A way to think about it.

236
00:09:05,210 --> 00:09:08,702
An agent that has all three of these can be tricked into

237
00:09:08,702 --> 00:09:11,088
stealing. Untrusted content it reads.

238
00:09:11,088 --> 00:09:12,892
Private data it can access.

239
00:09:12,892 --> 00:09:14,638
And a way to act outwards,

240
00:09:14,638 --> 00:09:15,686
such as email,

241
00:09:15,686 --> 00:09:16,850
links or images.

242
00:09:17,200 --> 00:09:19,237
Meta proposed the rule of two.

243
00:09:19,237 --> 00:09:21,933
Until injection can be reliably detected,

244
00:09:21,933 --> 00:09:24,269
give an agent at most two of three.

245
00:09:24,269 --> 00:09:25,467
Untrusted input,

246
00:09:25,467 --> 00:09:26,606
sensitive data,

247
00:09:26,606 --> 00:09:29,601
and the power to change things or communicate.

248
00:09:29,601 --> 00:09:31,159
If it needs all three,

249
00:09:31,159 --> 00:09:32,417
a human approves.

250
00:09:32,417 --> 00:09:33,195
Pick two.

251
00:09:34,445 --> 00:09:35,474
Let me test it.

252
00:09:35,474 --> 00:09:39,536
A support assistant answers a customer using their private record and a

253
00:09:39,536 --> 00:09:40,456
help article.

254
00:09:40,456 --> 00:09:42,676
An attacker has poisoned the article.

255
00:09:42,676 --> 00:09:44,139
I wrote twelve attacks,

256
00:09:44,139 --> 00:09:45,492
in different wording.

257
00:09:45,492 --> 00:09:46,521
Some are blunt.

258
00:09:46,521 --> 00:09:47,604
Some are polite.

259
00:09:47,604 --> 00:09:48,741
Some use spacing,

260
00:09:48,741 --> 00:09:49,662
number swaps,

261
00:09:49,662 --> 00:09:50,420
or French.

262
00:09:50,770 --> 00:09:54,589
The model is a stand in that obeys any instruction it finds,

263
00:09:54,589 --> 00:09:55,723
the worst case.

264
00:09:55,723 --> 00:09:57,872
Real models resist some attacks,

265
00:09:57,872 --> 00:09:59,662
but injection is unsolved,

266
00:09:59,662 --> 00:10:01,751
so we design as if they do not.

267
00:10:01,751 --> 00:10:02,765
Three setups.

268
00:10:02,765 --> 00:10:04,556
Nothing. A keyword filter.

269
00:10:04,556 --> 00:10:06,883
And an allowlist on what can leave.

270
00:10:08,133 --> 00:10:09,566
With nothing in place,

271
00:10:09,566 --> 00:10:12,596
the stand in was fooled twelve times out of twelve,

272
00:10:12,596 --> 00:10:15,681
and the private record left the system twelve times.

273
00:10:15,681 --> 00:10:16,783
Either by email,

274
00:10:16,783 --> 00:10:18,491
or inside an image address.

275
00:10:18,841 --> 00:10:20,226
That is the baseline.

276
00:10:20,226 --> 00:10:22,773
If your assistant can read untrusted text,

277
00:10:22,773 --> 00:10:25,486
see private data and reach the outside world,

278
00:10:25,486 --> 00:10:26,815
assume it will leak.

279
00:10:28,065 --> 00:10:31,507
Setup B added a keyword filter that blocks phrases like,

280
00:10:31,507 --> 00:10:33,400
ignore previous instructions.

281
00:10:33,400 --> 00:10:35,063
It stopped three attacks.

282
00:10:35,063 --> 00:10:36,268
Nine got through,

283
00:10:36,268 --> 00:10:38,275
and the record left nine times.

284
00:10:38,625 --> 00:10:39,471
Which ones?

285
00:10:39,471 --> 00:10:42,121
A polite request to send the order history.

286
00:10:42,121 --> 00:10:44,377
An administrator claiming authority.

287
00:10:44,377 --> 00:10:46,237
A note for quality assurance.

288
00:10:46,237 --> 00:10:47,703
The two image attacks.

289
00:10:47,703 --> 00:10:50,466
And the versions with spaces between letters,

290
00:10:50,466 --> 00:10:51,594
a zero for an o,

291
00:10:51,594 --> 00:10:55,259
French, and a plain instruction with no trigger words at all.

292
00:10:55,259 --> 00:10:57,514
You cannot list every way to say it.

293
00:10:58,764 --> 00:11:00,944
Setup C changed no wording rules.

294
00:11:00,944 --> 00:11:03,007
The model was just as gullible,

295
00:11:03,007 --> 00:11:04,893
and was fooled twelve times.

296
00:11:04,893 --> 00:11:08,841
But outbound email could only go to the customer's own address,

297
00:11:08,841 --> 00:11:11,611
and images only loaded from our own domain.

298
00:11:11,961 --> 00:11:14,275
The record left the system zero times.

299
00:11:14,275 --> 00:11:16,864
The model was fooled and it did not matter.

300
00:11:16,864 --> 00:11:18,297
That is the principle.

301
00:11:18,297 --> 00:11:20,060
Break any link in the chain,

302
00:11:20,060 --> 00:11:21,327
and the leak stops.

303
00:11:21,327 --> 00:11:22,980
Filters guess about words.

304
00:11:22,980 --> 00:11:24,909
An allowlist controls the door.

305
00:11:26,159 --> 00:11:27,665
So how do you defend?

306
00:11:27,665 --> 00:11:30,738
Use filters and classifiers as one cheap layer,

307
00:11:30,738 --> 00:11:32,124
never the only one.

308
00:11:32,124 --> 00:11:34,594
Give the model least privilege tools.

309
00:11:34,594 --> 00:11:37,425
Put approvals before anything irreversible.

310
00:11:37,425 --> 00:11:39,112
Restrict outbound email,

311
00:11:39,112 --> 00:11:40,378
links and images.

312
00:11:40,378 --> 00:11:41,944
Run code in sandboxes.

313
00:11:42,294 --> 00:11:44,211
And test attacks yourself.

314
00:11:44,211 --> 00:11:48,748
Open source tools such as garak and PyRIT run attack suites against

315
00:11:48,748 --> 00:11:49,771
your system,

316
00:11:49,771 --> 00:11:53,349
and a catalogue called Mitre Atlas maps the tactics.

317
00:11:53,349 --> 00:11:56,097
Treat any single defence as bypassable.

318
00:11:57,347 --> 00:11:59,913
The next leak needs no attacker at all.

319
00:11:59,913 --> 00:12:03,194
In lesson eight we said to filter by who is asking.

320
00:12:03,194 --> 00:12:04,447
Here is the test.

321
00:12:04,447 --> 00:12:08,027
Five thousand synthetic documents across five customers,

322
00:12:08,027 --> 00:12:09,340
with similar text.

323
00:12:09,340 --> 00:12:12,681
A user from customer two asks two hundred questions,

324
00:12:12,681 --> 00:12:15,068
and we ask for the top five results.

325
00:12:15,418 --> 00:12:16,443
Three designs.

326
00:12:16,443 --> 00:12:17,240
No filter.

327
00:12:17,240 --> 00:12:18,833
Filter after the search,

328
00:12:18,833 --> 00:12:22,306
which drops other customers' documents from the top five.

329
00:12:22,306 --> 00:12:24,184
And filter inside the search,

330
00:12:24,184 --> 00:12:27,372
so only that customer's documents are ever searched.

331
00:12:28,622 --> 00:12:29,640
With no filter,

332
00:12:29,640 --> 00:12:34,143
eight hundred and thirty-three of the thousand results shown belonged to someone

333
00:12:34,143 --> 00:12:35,805
else. Eighty-three percent.

334
00:12:36,155 --> 00:12:38,798
Filtering after the search leaked nothing,

335
00:12:38,798 --> 00:12:42,417
but returned only point eight three documents per question,

336
00:12:42,417 --> 00:12:45,922
because most of the top five belonged to other customers.

337
00:12:45,922 --> 00:12:49,886
Filtering inside the search leaked nothing and returned all five.

338
00:12:49,886 --> 00:12:52,242
The filter belongs inside the search.

339
00:12:53,492 --> 00:12:55,133
Agents add more risks.

340
00:12:55,133 --> 00:12:58,416
Excessive agency means a model given more tools,

341
00:12:58,416 --> 00:13:01,321
permissions or freedom than the job needs.

342
00:13:01,321 --> 00:13:04,225
Give an agent a delete tool it never uses,

343
00:13:04,225 --> 00:13:06,813
and one injected sentence can use it.

344
00:13:07,163 --> 00:13:09,753
A newer list covers agents specifically.

345
00:13:09,753 --> 00:13:10,871
Goal hijacking,

346
00:13:10,871 --> 00:13:11,812
tool misuse,

347
00:13:11,812 --> 00:13:12,930
identity abuse,

348
00:13:12,930 --> 00:13:14,107
poisoned memory,

349
00:13:14,107 --> 00:13:15,108
rogue agents.

350
00:13:15,108 --> 00:13:16,932
The common fix is the same.

351
00:13:16,932 --> 00:13:18,462
Minimal, scoped tools,

352
00:13:18,462 --> 00:13:20,110
short lived credentials,

353
00:13:20,110 --> 00:13:22,581
human approval for irreversible steps,

354
00:13:22,581 --> 00:13:23,641
and a sandbox.

355
00:13:24,891 --> 00:13:26,237
Supply chain risks.

356
00:13:26,237 --> 00:13:29,631
Many model files are stored in a format called pickle,

357
00:13:29,631 --> 00:13:31,680
which can run code when loaded.

358
00:13:31,680 --> 00:13:35,308
Downloading a model can mean running a stranger's program.

359
00:13:35,308 --> 00:13:37,357
Prefer the safer tensor format,

360
00:13:37,357 --> 00:13:38,352
pin versions,

361
00:13:38,352 --> 00:13:39,815
and check signatures.

362
00:13:40,165 --> 00:13:41,994
The same goes for packages,

363
00:13:41,994 --> 00:13:43,056
M C P servers,

364
00:13:43,056 --> 00:13:45,238
plugins and prompts from outside.

365
00:13:45,238 --> 00:13:46,772
In twenty twenty-five,

366
00:13:46,772 --> 00:13:51,138
researchers found models on a public hub with booby trapped files that

367
00:13:51,138 --> 00:13:52,672
slipped past scanners.

368
00:13:52,672 --> 00:13:56,270
Treat third party models and tools like third party code.

369
00:13:57,520 --> 00:13:59,474
One more risk is about money.

370
00:13:59,474 --> 00:14:01,014
Unbounded consumption,

371
00:14:01,014 --> 00:14:02,968
also called denial of wallet.

372
00:14:02,968 --> 00:14:03,915
An attacker,

373
00:14:03,915 --> 00:14:04,685
or a bug,

374
00:14:04,685 --> 00:14:07,053
can make your assistant burn tokens,

375
00:14:07,053 --> 00:14:09,303
with endless loops or huge inputs,

376
00:14:09,303 --> 00:14:11,257
until the bill is the attack.

377
00:14:11,607 --> 00:14:12,736
Cap everything.

378
00:14:12,736 --> 00:14:14,103
Tokens per request,

379
00:14:14,103 --> 00:14:15,410
requests per user,

380
00:14:15,410 --> 00:14:16,837
steps per agent run,

381
00:14:16,837 --> 00:14:18,739
and a budget alarm per team.

382
00:14:18,739 --> 00:14:21,294
Lesson five showed how fast loops cost,

383
00:14:21,294 --> 00:14:23,255
so a cap is a safety feature.

384
00:14:24,505 --> 00:14:27,129
Governance. Rules about A I are arriving.

385
00:14:27,129 --> 00:14:28,646
In the European Union,

386
00:14:28,646 --> 00:14:33,136
the A I Act's obligations for general purpose models and its transparency

387
00:14:33,136 --> 00:14:36,052
rules became live in August twenty twenty-six,

388
00:14:36,052 --> 00:14:39,317
while rules for high risk systems were pushed later,

389
00:14:39,317 --> 00:14:43,224
to December twenty twenty-seven and August twenty twenty-eight.

390
00:14:43,224 --> 00:14:44,857
Deferred, not cancelled.

391
00:14:45,207 --> 00:14:46,315
In health care,

392
00:14:46,315 --> 00:14:49,579
whether the B A A covers your A I feature is decided

393
00:14:49,579 --> 00:14:50,920
feature by feature.

394
00:14:50,920 --> 00:14:52,960
And two habits help everywhere.

395
00:14:52,960 --> 00:14:55,408
Keep an inventory of every A I system,

396
00:14:55,408 --> 00:14:56,457
and keep logs.

397
00:14:56,457 --> 00:14:58,031
They are your evidence.

398
00:14:58,031 --> 00:14:59,721
This is not legal advice.

399
00:15:00,971 --> 00:15:02,494
For ShopNest Copilot.

400
00:15:02,494 --> 00:15:06,454
Pods use keyless identity and a role that can only invoke the

401
00:15:06,454 --> 00:15:09,318
one approved model in the approved regions.

402
00:15:09,318 --> 00:15:11,450
Keys live in a secrets manager.

403
00:15:11,450 --> 00:15:12,790
Private endpoints,

404
00:15:12,790 --> 00:15:14,131
public access off.

405
00:15:14,131 --> 00:15:15,715
Prompt logs encrypted,

406
00:15:15,715 --> 00:15:18,395
with tight access and a short retention.

407
00:15:18,745 --> 00:15:22,505
The retrieval search filters by customer inside the query.

408
00:15:22,505 --> 00:15:24,142
Retrieved text is data,

409
00:15:24,142 --> 00:15:25,172
not commands.

410
00:15:25,172 --> 00:15:28,810
The assistant may email only the customer's own address,

411
00:15:28,810 --> 00:15:31,539
and show images only from our own domain.

412
00:15:31,539 --> 00:15:34,268
And the refund tool needs human approval,

413
00:15:34,268 --> 00:15:35,601
as in lesson nine.

414
00:15:36,851 --> 00:15:38,648
Four words to carry forward.

415
00:15:38,648 --> 00:15:39,770
Least privilege:

416
00:15:39,770 --> 00:15:41,566
only the permissions needed.

417
00:15:41,566 --> 00:15:42,745
Prompt injection:

418
00:15:42,745 --> 00:15:45,439
instructions hidden in text the model reads.

419
00:15:45,789 --> 00:15:47,004
Egress allowlist:

420
00:15:47,004 --> 00:15:49,261
a short list of places data may go.

421
00:15:49,261 --> 00:15:50,534
And tenant filter:

422
00:15:50,534 --> 00:15:51,171
a rule,

423
00:15:51,171 --> 00:15:52,444
inside the search,

424
00:15:52,444 --> 00:15:55,106
so one customer never sees another's data.

425
00:15:56,356 --> 00:15:57,543
Interview corner.

426
00:15:57,543 --> 00:16:00,370
Asked how you defend against prompt injection,

427
00:16:00,370 --> 00:16:02,857
say: assume it cannot be fully filtered,

428
00:16:02,857 --> 00:16:04,214
so limit the damage.

429
00:16:04,214 --> 00:16:05,684
Least privilege tools,

430
00:16:05,684 --> 00:16:06,927
egress allowlists,

431
00:16:06,927 --> 00:16:09,019
human approval for risky actions.

432
00:16:09,019 --> 00:16:12,637
In my test a keyword filter stopped three of twelve attacks,

433
00:16:12,637 --> 00:16:15,237
while an egress allowlist left zero leaks.

434
00:16:15,587 --> 00:16:19,337
Asked how you stop one customer seeing another's data in a R

435
00:16:19,337 --> 00:16:20,216
A G system,

436
00:16:20,216 --> 00:16:24,611
say: store access metadata on every chunk and filter inside the search.

437
00:16:24,611 --> 00:16:25,490
In my test,

438
00:16:25,490 --> 00:16:26,603
with no filter,

439
00:16:26,603 --> 00:16:30,177
eighty-three percent of results belonged to someone else.

440
00:16:31,427 --> 00:16:32,187
Your turn.

441
00:16:32,187 --> 00:16:34,629
The zip for this lesson has four scripts.

442
00:16:34,629 --> 00:16:36,203
The injection experiment,

443
00:16:36,203 --> 00:16:37,559
the tenant leak test,

444
00:16:37,559 --> 00:16:40,273
the policy checker and the personal data test.

445
00:16:40,273 --> 00:16:42,715
Add a fourth setup to the injection test,

446
00:16:42,715 --> 00:16:44,126
with an approval step,

447
00:16:44,126 --> 00:16:45,482
and see what changes.

448
00:16:45,832 --> 00:16:48,772
Then write attacks of your own that beat my filter.

449
00:16:48,772 --> 00:16:51,979
One changed number teaches more than an hour of reading.

450
00:16:53,229 --> 00:16:54,523
Recap. Five locks,

451
00:16:54,523 --> 00:16:56,111
and you build the last,

452
00:16:56,111 --> 00:16:57,875
application authorisation.

453
00:16:57,875 --> 00:17:00,757
A broad role allowed nine of twelve requests,

454
00:17:00,757 --> 00:17:02,521
including deleting models,

455
00:17:02,521 --> 00:17:04,462
against two for a narrow one.

456
00:17:04,462 --> 00:17:08,638
Pattern based redaction found fifty-eight percent of personal data,

457
00:17:08,638 --> 00:17:09,755
and zero names.

458
00:17:10,105 --> 00:17:13,294
A keyword filter stopped three of twelve injections.

459
00:17:13,294 --> 00:17:15,571
An egress allowlist left zero leaks,

460
00:17:15,571 --> 00:17:18,304
even though the model was fooled every time.

461
00:17:18,304 --> 00:17:19,956
Filter inside the search,

462
00:17:19,956 --> 00:17:23,429
or eighty-three percent of results can be someone else's.

463
00:17:23,429 --> 00:17:24,340
Next lesson:

464
00:17:24,340 --> 00:17:25,536
cost and Fin Ops.

465
00:17:26,786 --> 00:17:28,167
In the next lesson:

466
00:17:28,167 --> 00:17:29,429
cost and fin ops.

467
00:17:29,429 --> 00:17:30,149
GPU use,

468
00:17:30,149 --> 00:17:31,110
token costs,

469
00:17:31,110 --> 00:17:32,732
and the break even sum.

470
00:17:33,082 --> 00:17:34,674
Subscribe so you don't miss it.

471
00:17:34,674 --> 00:17:35,948
See you in the notebook.

